## Upgrade compatibility

Morfi Verde is deployed through the existing `asadosverde` chart. The chart name, helper names, Kubernetes resource names, bundled PostgreSQL database and user, Secret names, and PVC names remain `asadosverde` for upgrade compatibility. Upgrade existing releases in place with the same Helm release name.

## Production setup

The default application image is the private Morfi Verde GHCR package `ghcr.io/sebagarayco/morfiverde`. Create a registry Secret and reference it with `imagePullSecrets`:

```shell
kubectl create secret docker-registry registry-credentials \
  --docker-server=ghcr.io \
  --docker-username='<github-user>' \
  --docker-password='<github-token>'
```

Create a separate application Secret and set `app.existingSecret`. The container requires `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_NAME`, `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL`, and `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD` on every startup. `BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_NICKNAME` is optional. The same Secret can contain `VERIFICATION_CODE_PEPPER`, `RESEND_API_KEY`, `RESEND_FROM_EMAIL`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and other sensitive application environment variables. Do not put their values in a values file.

The image entrypoint applies Prisma migrations and runs the idempotent seed before starting the server. This chart deliberately does not create a duplicate migration Job. Keep `app.command` and `app.args` empty unless using a test image.

## Vote reminders

The optional `voteReminder` CronJob invokes the application's protected internal endpoint once per hour. It is disabled by default because the chart does not provision its bearer token. To enable it, create a Kubernetes Secret containing the token, set `voteReminder.secret.existingSecret`, and keep the token itself out of values files. The CronJob reads only the configured key through a `secretKeyRef`; it does not embed or print the token.

The application owns the local reminder hour, Buenos Aires eligibility rules, and same-day deduplication. The CronJob therefore remains reusable and only supplies a reliable hourly trigger. `voteReminder.timeZone` defaults to `America/Argentina/Buenos_Aires` and requires Kubernetes 1.27 or newer. Set it to an empty string on older clusters to omit the CronJob `timeZone` field.

## Azure Key Vault secret synchronization

The chart can optionally create akv2k8s `AzureKeyVaultSecret` resources. It does not install the CRD or controller and does not provision Azure resources. The feature is disabled by default, so `app.existingSecret`, `postgresql.auth.existingSecret`, `externalDatabase.existingSecret`, and `imagePullSecrets` continue to support manually provisioned Kubernetes Secrets.

Set `azureKeyVaultSecrets.apiVersion` to a version served by the installed CRD. The default is the current upstream `spv.no/v2beta1`; override it when the cluster serves another version. Resource names must be unique. Entries inherit `azureKeyVaultSecrets.vaultName` and can override it with `vaultName`. They can also select an Azure identity when the selected CRD version supports it, pin an object version, apply the ordered `trim`, `base64encode`, and `base64decode` transforms, and configure the output Secret type, data key, or `ensureserverfirst` certificate chain order.

For `multi-key-value-secret`, store one JSON or YAML object in Key Vault and set the matching `contentType`; its keys become Kubernetes Secret keys, so `dataKey` is omitted. Point `app.existingSecret` at the configured output Secret name when the object contains application environment variables.

To materialize private-registry credentials, use output type `kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson`, set `dataKey` to `.dockerconfigjson`, and reference the output Secret with `imagePullSecrets`. The Key Vault object must contain the raw, valid Docker config JSON payload, not an already base64-encoded Kubernetes Secret:

```json
{
  "auths": {
    "registry.example.com": {
      "username": "<registry-user>",
      "password": "<registry-token>",
      "auth": "<base64-of-username-colon-token>"
    }
  }
}
```

The controller can generate `auth` when it is omitted. Helm does not wait for the controller to materialize output Secrets, so deployment orchestration must account for that asynchronous boundary.

## Database modes

PostgreSQL is enabled by default. The chart creates a stable headless Service, a single-replica StatefulSet, and a standalone chart-managed PVC mounted at the official image data root with `PGDATA` in a subdirectory. Keeping the claim outside `volumeClaimTemplates` allows supported size expansion without changing immutable StatefulSet fields. When `postgresql.auth.existingSecret` is empty, Helm generates an alphanumeric password and stores both `postgres-password` and `database-url` in a release Secret. `lookup` preserves the password on upgrades, and both PostgreSQL and the application read the same Secret.

To manage credentials externally, set `postgresql.auth.existingSecret`. That Secret must contain the keys configured by `postgresql.auth.passwordKey` and `postgresql.auth.databaseUrlKey`, and the URL must use the same password as the password key.

To use external PostgreSQL, disable the bundled database and reference a Secret containing the complete Prisma-compatible URL:

```yaml
postgresql:
  enabled: false
externalDatabase:
  existingSecret: asadosverde-external-db
  existingSecretKey: database-url
```

Rendering fails when PostgreSQL is disabled without both external Secret settings.

## Persistence

Uploaded profile images are written to `/app/uploads` and use a PVC by default. PostgreSQL uses its own stable standalone PVC, or the claim named by `postgresql.persistence.existingClaim`. Empty storage class values use the cluster default and keep the chart portable. Set either storage class explicitly when required by the target cluster.

PVC storage classes and access modes are immutable after creation. Changing either requires provisioning a replacement claim and migrating data, or selecting a prepared claim with `existingClaim`. Increasing `postgresql.persistence.size` is supported only when the selected StorageClass and CSI driver allow volume expansion; shrinking a PVC is not supported by Kubernetes.

## CI installation

`ci/ct-values.yaml` replaces the private application image with public unprivileged nginx, adjusts ports and probes, and disables upload persistence. Chart-testing automatically discovers this values file and can therefore exercise installation without weakening production defaults:

```shell
ct install --config ../../ct.yaml --charts .
```

